WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:14.730
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. A quick warning to everybody who's listening to this podcast version of our show. There are some words that we have unbeeped. If you don't want to hear that, maybe you're listening with kids, you can get a beeped version at our website, ThisAmericanLife.org.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。向所有收听我们节目播客版的朋友做个简短的警告：节目中有一些我们没有消音的词汇。如果你不想听到这些，比如你正和孩子一起收听，可以在我们的网站 ThisAmericanLife.org 获取消音版。

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:22.440
<v Ira Glass>So Chana Joffe-Walt, one of our producers, has been reporting this story. It's going be the whole hour today. It's a really good one. Here she is.
<v Ira Glass>我们的制作人之一恰娜·乔夫-沃尔特报道了这个故事。这将占据今天的整整一个小时。这是一个非常精彩的故事。她来了。

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:35.700
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When Vivian started reading about the men in Hollywood, and the media, and their sexual transgressions, she thought of the men in her past who had behaved poorly, the man who assaulted her in her 20s, and the others--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在回家的路上，她又想到了安全套的事。迪娜关于唐的故事——他是个好人、一个成熟的、自我实现的、为她所倾倒的男人——其实也是一个关于她自己的故事。她很有力量，很有趣，能在重要议题上改变他的主意。而他甚至从未想过要问一句关于防护措施的事——

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:47.760
<v Vivian>A few unwanted kisses over the years-- I mean, truly unwanted, drunken guys just sort of pushing themselves on me, and, you know, the usual array of jokes and comments, and this and that.
<v Vivian>这些年来一些不受欢迎的亲吻——我的意思是，真正不受欢迎的，喝醉的家伙只是把他们自己强加在我身上，你知道的，还有一如既往的各种玩笑和评论，诸如此类。

00:00:47.760 --> 00:01:01.980
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>She thought of them, and she thought of Don, her partner of 23 years. And Vivian wondered. Don was a boss to dozens of women. He ran AlterNet, a progressive news website, and he was a flirt.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她想到了他们，她想到了唐，她陪伴了23年的伴侣。薇薇安不禁犯嘀咕。唐对几十名女性来说是个老板。他经营着AlterNet一个渐进式新闻网站，而且他很喜欢调情。

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:16.440
<v Vivian>It was easy for me to imagine that Don had missed unspoken signals-- being a little too present, a little too close, a little too eager, a little too-- you know, those kinds of things.
<v Vivian>我很容易想象，唐错过了那些没有说出口的信号——表现得太显眼、太靠近、太急切、太——你知道的，诸如此类的事情。

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:22.830
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>She'd seen it. She'd seen Don's attention make women uncomfortable at parties, in restaurants.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她亲眼见过。她见过唐的注意力在聚会和餐厅里让女性感到不舒服。

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:33.290
<v Vivian>And what would end up happening is I would catch her eye, and we would roll our eyes together-- like oh, God, men --there would be a sort of a bond there, and things would go on.
<v Vivian>最后往往会变成我会跟她对上眼神，然后我们俩一起翻白眼——就像“天哪，男人”一样，我们之间会有一种默契，然后事情就过去了。

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:37.840
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And this is like Don flirting with some woman in front of you.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这就像唐当着你的面跟别的女人调情。

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:38.850
<v Vivian>Yeah.
<v Vivian>对。

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:39.780
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He was a flirt?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他很爱调情？

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:47.820
<v Vivian>Yes, I knew he was a flirt. I also really knew that that didn't have anything to do with us. That had to do with a need for attention.
<v Vivian>是的，我知道他爱调情。我也非常清楚这跟我们没有任何关系。这跟对关注的需求有关。

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Did you feel embarrassed by it?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你为此感到尴尬吗?

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:57.030
<v Ira Glass>Of course, of course. But it pass quickly. It was usually very easy to move things along.
<v Ira Glass>当然，当然。但它很快就过去了。事情通常很容易就翻篇。

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:03.450
<v Vivian>Then I could step in. I could step between. I could engage her in conversation, whatever, right.
<v Vivian>然后我就可以介入。我可以插在中间。我可以把她拉进对话中，不管怎样，对吧。

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.680
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Would you talk to Don about it?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你会和唐谈这事吗？

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:10.900
<v Vivian>Rarely, because I didn't think I'd be believed.
<v Vivian>很少，因为我觉得他不会相信我。

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:11.690
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>By him.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他不会信。

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:24.670
<v Vivian>By him. I think he'd sort of laugh it off, and say, oh, you just don't get it. It's all in fun-- which I do think was his attitude-- which I know was his attitude.
<v Vivian>他不会信。我想他会一笑置之，然后说，噢，你就是不懂。这只是开玩笑——我确实觉得这就是他的态度——我知道这就是他的态度。

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:38.440
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So a couple of months ago, when Don told Vivian that BuzzFeed News was writing a story about him, Vivian was not surprised. People who used to work for Don at AlterNet were accusing Don of sexually harassing them.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以几个月前，当唐告诉薇薇安《BuzzFeed新闻》正在写一篇关于他的报道时，薇薇安并不感到惊讶。以前在AlterNet为唐工作过的人指控唐对她们进行性骚扰。

00:02:38.440 --> 00:03:00.580
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Vivian read the article. A number of women detailed encounters with Don-- unwanted advances, inappropriate touching, sending them explicit e-mails. Vivian recognized Don in some parts; others felt like they were describing a person she'd never met. Mostly, she had a particular experience reading that story that actually had nothing to do with Don.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>薇薇安读了那篇文章。许多女性详细描述了与唐的遭遇——不请自来的求爱、不适当的触摸、发送露骨的电子邮件。薇薇安在某些部分认出了唐；而其他部分感觉就像是在描述一个她从未见过的人。最主要的是，她在阅读那篇报道时有一种特殊的体验，而这实际上与唐毫无关系。

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:06.760
<v Vivian>A memory came back to me from when I was 28 that I'm sure I hadn't thought about since it happened.
<v Vivian>我记起了一段我28岁时的记忆，我确信自从事情发生后我就再没想过它。

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:14.770
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>She was training to be a psychologist with a bunch of young trainees at a clinic. She was in the back room where the staff took breaks and wrote notes.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她当时正在一家诊所里和一群年轻的实习生一起接受心理学培训。她在后面的房间里，员工们在那里休息和写笔记。

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:24.550
<v Vivian>The supervisor was a man, as virtually all the supervisors at the time were, in his 40s, maybe even early 50s.
<v Vivian>主管是个男人，几乎当时所有的主管都是这样，40多岁，甚至可能快50岁。

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:37.590
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Vivian was on the couch with a friend, a woman in her 40s, and the two of them watched as many of the young psychology interns, women Vivian's age, in their 20s, crowded around the supervisor.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>薇薇安和一个朋友——一个40多岁的女人——坐在沙发上，她们俩看着许多年轻的心理学实习生，也就是薇薇安这个年纪、20多岁的女性，挤在主管周围。

00:03:37.590 --> 00:04:08.150
<v Vivian>They're surrounding him, and sort of hanging on his words, and leaning in, and it's just got that-- no offense, his words weren't that interesting, you know. It's like, they were fine. But, oh my God, and he was eating it up. You could just see he loved this. And then I'm sitting in the back of the room, on the couch with this other woman, Lynn, and she looks up and she goes, hm, the cupcakes.
<v Vivian>她们包围着他，几乎全神贯注地听着他的话，身体向前倾，那种感觉——无意冒犯，他的话其实没那么有趣，你知道的。就像，它们还行。但是，天哪，而且他非常享受。你能看出他太喜欢这个了。然后我坐在房间后面，和另一个叫琳的女人坐在沙发上，她抬起头说，哼，那些小蛋糕。

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:14.060
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Vivian lost it. She loved this word. It was perfect.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>薇薇安笑疯了。她太喜欢这个词了。简直绝了。

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:18.280
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And you understood immediately that that was meant to refer to the women who were surrounding the supervisor.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你当时立刻就明白那是用来指代那些包围着主管的女人们。

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:29.540
<v Vivian>Absolutely. Absolutely. And you know, and I was really proud that she was basically saying, you're not one of them. You know, I did not want to be one of them.
<v Vivian>绝对是的。绝对是。你知道的，我真的很骄傲，因为她基本上是在说，你可不是她们中的一员。你知道的，我才不想成为她们中的一员。

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.170
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>She was basically saying that you're cool and they're not.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她基本上是在说你很酷，而她们不是。

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:39.610
<v Vivian>Exactly, exactly. Like we're grown up women, and they're not. Oh, God, yeah. That's who I want to be.
<v Vivian>没错，没错。就像我们是成年女性，而她们不是。哦，天哪，是的。这正是我想要成为的人。

00:04:39.610 --> 00:05:01.420
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That is where her mind when reading about her partner of two decades in the news-- the cupcakes. Vivian felt full of regret. She regretted her contempt for the cupcakes. She regretted her acceptance that this was the way it would always be between men and women. Men would have power, and women would have to deal with that.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这就是当她在新闻中读到关于她陪伴了二十年的伴侣时她的脑海所想的——小蛋糕。薇薇安充满了遗憾。她后悔自己对那些小蛋糕的蔑视。她后悔自己接受了男女之间本该如此的现状。男人掌握权力，而女人不得不去应付这一点。

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:20.910
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What I found so interesting about this was, for Vivian, learning the news about Don sparked this memory that was not about Don, but in some ways it felt like it had everything to do with Don. It explained something to her about who she is, and how she is, and choices and assumptions she's made in her life.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我发现这最有趣的地方在于，对薇薇安来说，得知关于唐的消息触发了这段与唐无关的记忆，但在某种程度上，它又感觉与唐密不可分。它向她解释了关于她自己是谁、她是怎么样的，以及她在生活中做出的选择和假设。

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:54.930
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Now I want to tell you about another group of people I've been talking to, the women who worked for Don, including women who say they were harassed by him. I talked with them, and they each told me their Don stories, but they also did the same thing Vivian did. They brought up specific experiences from their past, things that happened years ago. Because, for them, that stuff in the past feels related to their experiences with Don.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>现在我想跟你们讲讲另一群我一直在交流的人，那些为唐工作的女性，包括那些自称遭到他骚扰的女性。我和她们交谈过，她们每个人都向我讲述了她们与唐的故事，但她们也做了和薇薇安一样的事情。她们提到了自己过去的特定经历，那些多年前发生的事情。因为对她们来说，过去那些事感觉与她们和唐的遭遇息息相关。

00:05:54.930 --> 00:06:19.750
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Over the last few months of Me Too stories, I keep wanting to hear more from the women who were in the news reports, their broader history, not just the bad experience they had with one man at an office or on a movie set, but the other moments before the harassment, because there is no Me Too moment that is actually separate from the rest of our lives.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在过去几个月的“我也是”运动故事中，我一直想要听到更多来自新闻报道中女性的声音，了解她们更广泛的经历，不仅仅是她们在办公室或片场与某个男人有过的不愉快经历，还有骚扰发生前的其他时刻，因为没有任何一个“我也是”的时刻是真正与我们生活的其余部分隔绝的。

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:40.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So today's show, we're going to do that. Five women tell their stories, Vivian and four women who worked for her partner, Don Hazen, over the course of many years, at different times, with a wide range of experiences. We're going to hear from each of them one at a time about their experiences with Don, and their experiences before Don.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以今天的节目，我们要这么做。五位女性讲述她们的故事，薇薇安和四位曾在不同时期、历经多年、有着广泛丰富经历的、为她的伴侣唐·哈森工作的女性。我们将依次倾听她们每个人关于她们与唐的经历，以及在遇到唐之前的经历。

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:56.630
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Don resigned from his job a few months ago, and told me the board that oversees AlterNet insisted he couldn't talk on the record. I can tell you some about his version of events, but you won't be hearing from him. It's not a story about him, it's about them, these five women.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>唐几个月前辞去了工作，他告诉我，监督AlterNet的董事会坚持认为他不能公开接受采访。我可以告诉你一些他经历的版本，但你不会听到他亲口诉说。这不是一个关于他的故事，而是关于她们，这五个女性。

00:06:56.630 --> 00:07:18.210
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What these women are doing is what I think so many of us have been doing lately. They're doing it in ways I found brave and vulnerable. They're re-evaluating who they are, how they've related to men, how they came to think of certain things as normal. It's a reckoning, not just for men, for women.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这些女性正在做的事情，正是我想象中我们许多人最近一直在做的事情。她们以一种让我觉得勇敢而又脆弱的方式在做这件事。她们在重新评估自己是谁、自己是如何与男性相处的、自己是如何开始认为某些事情是正常的。这是一场清算，不仅是对男人的清算，也是对女人的清算。

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:34.120
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's This American Life. I'm Chana Joffe-Walt. Stay with us.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这里是《美国生活》。我是恰娜·乔夫-沃尔特。请继续收听。

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:48.340
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is probably evident by now, but just to say, we will talk about sex in this show, not just its existence, but some of the specifics. And there are some moments of abuse, so take this as a heads up. OK.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这现在可能已经很明显了，但还是要说一下，我们将在节目中讨论性，不仅是它的存在，还有一些具体细节。其中会有一些虐待的时刻，所以请先把这当作一个心理准备。好的。

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:54.250
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The women who worked for Don at AlterNet, beginning with Act 1, "Deanna."
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那些在AlterNet为唐工作的女性，从第一幕“迪娜”开始。

00:07:54.250 --> 00:08:08.050
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The list started when Deanna was eight years old. The first time it was Face from the A-Team. Deanna got a postcard of him that year when her family went to Universal Studios. She put a puffy red heart sticker on it, took it with her during the day--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这张名单始于迪娜八岁的时候。第一次是《天龙特攻队》里的“面具”。那年她随家人去环球影城时得到了一张他的明信片。她在上面贴了一个蓬松的红心贴纸，白天带着它——

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:17.980
<v Deanna>--slept with it at night, and I actually-- and I'm eight. And I felt like I was going to die if I never met him. I actually thought I would die. I was like, I will cease to exist if I don't get to meet this person.
<v Deanna>——晚上抱着它睡觉，而且我当时——我才八岁。我觉得如果我从未见过他，我就会死掉。我真的觉得自己会死。我想，如果我见不到这个人，我就会不复存在。

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:32.860
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The next one made her cry. He was 12, so was she. Deanna called her Aunt Rose from the basement, distressed. She'd written him letters, dedicated a song to him on the radio, and nothing, no response. Aunt Rose was quick to deliver advice.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>下一个把她弄哭了。他12岁，她也是。迪娜在地下室给罗斯阿姨打电话，感到很沮丧。她给他写了信，在广播里为他点了一首歌，却什么都没有，毫无回应。罗斯阿姨很快给出了建议。

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:48.720
<v Deanna>This is the way boys are. They don't understand you. They are dumb. They do not get you. You're strong and smart and independent, and that just is never going to make sense to men.
<v Deanna>男孩就是这样的。他们不懂你。他们很蠢。他们不理解你。你坚强、聪明、独立，而这一点对男人来说永远也说不通。

00:08:48.720 --> 00:09:02.770
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Aunt Rose explained it would be Deanna's job to make sense of things to men. If she was going to have relationships with them, it would never be easy, and it would be on her to figure it out. Boys were too dumb to do it for themselves.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>罗斯阿姨解释说，让男人搞懂事情将是迪娜的工作。如果她要和他们谈恋爱，这绝不会是一件容易的事，而且得靠她自己去摸索明白。男孩们太笨了，自己搞不懂。

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:09.130
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Jay's dad was a minister. Jay liked Monty Python, and had a car, and very few opinions.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>杰的爸爸是个牧师。杰喜欢巨蟒剧团，有一辆车，没有什么主见。

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:18.490
<v Deanna>He was incredibly sweet, and he was very much whatever you want to do. Well, what do you want to do? You know, not super-motivated on his own. I was like, come along with me.
<v Deanna>他好得难以置信，而且非常随和，你说做什么都行。好吧，你想做什么？你知道的，他自己不是特别有动力。我当时就想，跟我一起来吧。

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:28.030
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When Deanna went away to college, Jay said, I'll come, too. When she broke up with him, his mom called her mom to ask if there was any way they could get back together.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当迪娜去外地上大学时，杰说，我也要去。当她和他分手时，他妈妈给她妈妈打电话，问有没有什么办法他们能复合。

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:44.350
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Chris worked at the dollar store in the mall. Chris wanted more from his life. Deanna was 19, and got him a job on the other side of the mall at a Sam Goody she used to work at. She wanted more for him, too. He was funny, and moody, and he was the first person Deanna had sex with.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>克里斯在商场的美元店工作。克里斯想要从生活中得到更多。迪娜19岁时，在商场另一头她曾经工作过的一家Sam Goody唱片行为他找了一份工作。她也希望他能有更多成就。他既幽默又喜怒无常，而且是迪娜第一个与之发生性关系的男人。

00:09:44.350 --> 00:10:16.380
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Mark wanted a job in film or TV, but nobody wanted to give him film or TV jobs. It was his idea to move to New York, and once they were there, Deanna got a job first as a bookkeeper. Mark got no job. She called his friends, his friends' friends, and found Mark an IT job, which he hated, so she found him another job at a magazine, and he worked there for the next 10 years. Deanna moved out three weeks before 9/11.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>马克想在电影或电视行业找工作，但没人愿意给他电影或电视的工作。搬到纽约是他的主意，一旦到了那里，迪娜先找到了一份记账员的工作。马克没有工作。她给他的朋友、朋友的朋友打电话，为马克找了一份IT工作，他讨厌这份工作，所以她又帮他在一家杂志社找了另一份工作，他在那里工作了整整10年。迪娜在911事件发生前三周搬了出去。

