<v Laura>Yeah. Yeah, they were sitting right next to each other in a tight-- you know, it was a big table, but a crowded restaurant. So they were sitting very close together, and she was just, like, leaning over, like it was her plate.
<v Laura>对。对，他们紧挨着坐在一起——你知道的，那是一张大桌子，但是一家拥挤的餐厅。所以他们坐得很近，她就那样身体前倾，好像那是她自己的盘子一样。

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:36.040
<v Laura>But I mean, it's not normal. It's just not normal. It's just like, why would he need that? Why would she need to do that? Why was he letting her do that? Did he ask her to do it? I don't know. I mean, there was something about it that was just very profoundly disturbing.
<v Laura>但我的意思是，这很不正常。这根本不正常。这就像，他为什么需要别人这么做？她为什么要这么做？他为什么允许她这么做？是他让她这么做的吗？我不知道。我的意思是，这其中有些东西非常深刻地让人感到不安。

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:40.110
<v Onnesha>Oh, it was so weird. Oh, she's, like, cutting his meat for him.
<v Onnesha>哦，太诡异了。哦，她竟然在帮他切肉。

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:46.530
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Onnesha eventually noticed it, too, and she could not figure out what is up with this woman.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>奥内莎最终也注意到了这一点，她怎么也想不明白这个女人到底是怎么回事。

00:21:46.530 --> 00:22:05.250
<v Onnesha>I just don't understand this dynamic. Like, the manual I had constructed on how to navigate and deal with Don, I was just like flipping through all the pages, I was like, nope, nope, nope, what, no. I have no guide for this, where to place this behavior.
<v Onnesha>我就是不理解这种动态。就像，我为如何应付和对付唐而建立的那本手册，我当时在疯狂翻阅所有的页面，我想着，不对，不对，不对，什么，没有。我没有任何关于这个的指南，不知道该把这种行为归到哪一类。

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:07.230
