00:22:55.170 --> 00:23:08.430
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Most other girls quit being friends with boys earlier, but Onnesha was 13, and she still liked her friends. That summer, she hung out with the same boys she always had, in the same places-- the kitchen, her friend's basement playroom, or--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>大多数女孩很早就放弃和男孩做朋友了，但奥内莎13岁时，她依然喜欢她的朋友们。那个夏天，她和往常一样，在相同的地方和同一群男孩一起玩——厨房、她朋友的地下室游戏室，或者——

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:20.340
<v Onnesha>We would all go to the pool together, and I had this tie dyed bathing suit, this one-piece. And you know, like, most of my other friends were, like, wearing bikinis. But you know, it was like, no, I'm good.
<v Onnesha>我们会一起去游泳池，我当时穿着一件扎染的连体泳衣。你知道的，比如，我其他大部分朋友，都穿着比基尼。但你知道的，就像，不用了，我这样挺好。

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:22.560
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>At the snack bar, they would get popsicles.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在小吃部，他们会买冰棒。

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:43.310
<v Onnesha>You know, there would always be, like, talk about the scene at the pool, you know, like, talking about different girls, that would occasionally be part of a conversation. I think they probably reined it in when I was around, but there would occasionally be comments. And so they were, like, they had named someone The Hypnotizer. Like, there was this title for some woman.
<v Onnesha>你知道的，总是会有人，比如，谈论泳池边的景象，你知道的，比如讨论不同的女孩，这偶尔会成为对话的一部分。我想当我在场时他们大概有所收敛，但偶尔还是会有评论。所以他们，比如，给某个人起名叫“催眠师”。就像，某个女性有一个这样的头衔。

00:23:43.310 --> 00:23:49.920
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It would have been nothing if it was like the other girls they talked about, a passing thing. But this one persisted for weeks.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>如果这就像他们谈论的其他女孩那样，只是转瞬即逝的事，那倒也无所谓。但这个绰号持续了好几个星期。

00:23:49.920 --> 00:24:08.460
<v Onnesha>It got to a point, I recall, where they talked about it enough that I was like, oh, who is she? You know, I wanted to be in on it. I was, like, show her to me, you know. And they were all cryptic about it. They were like, oh The Hypnotizer, The Hypnotizer. Oh, she's here. Or, like, did you see The Hypnotizer. And I can't remember how I found out, but eventually I found out it was me.
<v Onnesha>我记得，事情发展到后来，他们讨论得足够多，以至于我心想，哦，她是谁？你知道的，我也想加入进去。我当时想，让我见见她，你知道的。而他们对此都遮遮掩掩的。他们会说，哦，催眠师，催眠师。哦，她来了。或者，嘿，你看到催眠师了吗。我记不清我是怎么发现的了，但最终我发现那个人其实是我。

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:19.830
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It was the first time Onnesha had the feeling. There was her, and there was someone else she hadn't known was there, a person who looked good to these boys, her friends.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是奥内莎第一次有这种感觉。这里有她，还有另一个她之前不知道存在的人——一个在这些男孩、她的朋友们眼中看起来很有魅力的存在。

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:45.840
<v Onnesha>I guess a part of me was pleased by that, but the more prevalent feeling was like whiplash. They were talking about me in front of me. It's like there was the me that was present and there and interacting with them, and then there was this other separate, objectified body. It just feels like this whole other person enters the room.
<v Onnesha>我想我内心的一部分对此感到高兴，但更普遍的感觉就像是遭到了一记鞭笞。他们当着我的面在谈论我。这就好像存在着当时在场、活着并与他们互动的我，然后又存在着另一个独立的、被客体化的躯体。这感觉就像是完全另一个人走进了房间。

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:46.980
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Another person who is you?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>另一个人就是你？

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:57.240
<v Onnesha>Yeah. Well, or I guess, fundamentally, I felt the same. But suddenly, I had to reckon with the fact that I did not look the same to other people.
<v Onnesha>对。嗯，或者我猜，从根本上说，我觉得自己没变。但突然之间，我不得不接受这样一个事实：在其他人眼中，我看起来已经不一样了。

00:24:57.240 --> 00:25:31.270
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It was obvious to Onnesha how to reckon with that. She needed to crush that other person. She did not want to wear the tie dye bathing suit ever again. She slouched. She wore baggy clothes. It wasn't like a decision or anything, it was just instinct. She watched the girls in bikinis that summer, and considered if they somehow had the opposite instinct, or maybe they had made a decision to actively try to merge the two girls, the one with breasts and the one from before. It really seemed easy for some of them, like they welcomed it.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>对于如何接受这一点，奥内莎心知肚明。她需要粉碎那个另一个人。她再也不想穿那件扎染泳衣了。她开始驼背。她穿肥大的衣服。这不像是经过了什么深思熟虑的决定，纯粹是出于本能。那个夏天，她看着那些穿比基尼的女孩，琢磨着她们是否不知何故拥有相反的本能，或者她们或许做出了决定，试图积极地将两个女孩合二为一——那个拥有胸部的女孩和以前那个女孩。对她们中的一些人来说，这似乎真的很容易，就像她们对此欣然接受一样。

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:53.700
<v Onnesha>And a part of me wishes that I could do that, sort of own it, you know, and, like, use it like a power. And it's just never struck me as a power. It feels like a false power. It feels like if that is somehow getting me attention, like it could turn. Like, that's not a safe kind of power to traffic in.
<v Onnesha>我内心的一部分真希望我也能做到那样，坦然接受它，你知道的，然后把它当成一种力量来使用。但这从来没有让我觉得那是一种力量。这感觉像是一种虚假的力量。这感觉就像，如果这不知何故为我赢得了关注，情况随时可能会逆转。这不是一种可以安全经手的力量。

00:25:53.700 --> 00:26:25.250
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>By the time Onnesha met Don, she wouldn't have been able to say if wearing loose clothing was her fashion sense or strategy. She was 23 years old. She'd been guarding against that separate person, The Hypnotizer, for a decade by that point. AlterNet was Onnesha's first real job, the first time she had a desk that was hers, the first time she learned a male colleague was getting paid more than her for the same work, the first time she asked for a raise from her boss, Don, which did not go how Onnesha expected.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>到奥内莎遇到唐的时候，她已经分不清穿宽松服装究竟是她的时尚品味还是某种策略了。她当时23岁。从那时起，她已经防范那个独立的个体——“催眠师”整整十年了。AlterNet是奥内莎第一份真正的工作，第一次拥有属于她自己的办公桌，第一次得知一个男同事因为同样的工作拿到的薪水比她高，第一次向她的老板唐要求加薪，而结果却出乎奥内莎的意料。

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:47.030
<v Onnesha>Yeah, so I, like, build this whole case, and I get in there, and I'm nervous. I have my opening foray where I'm just like, you know, I've been doing x and y, and the quality of the work I've been doing, how I've been working really hard, and I'm like, you know, and it's hard to survive in this city on such a low salary.
<v Onnesha>是的，所以，我，比如，罗列了所有的论据，然后我走进去，我很紧张。我进行了开场白，我只是说，你知道的，我一直在做X和Y，我所做工作的质量，我工作有多么努力，然后我说，你知道的，拿着这么低的薪水在这个城市里生活真的很艰难。

00:26:47.030 --> 00:27:16.890
<v Onnesha>He just, like, latched onto that. And he kind of leaned in, and was like, oh, are you having trouble paying rent? And I remember that so specifically, because it was like, my rent. It was so oddly personal, and unnecessary, and out of left field, and I was completely disarmed. And I didn't know how to respond. And I think I just sort of stuttered, and was like, no, it's not that.
<v Onnesha>他直接，比如，抓住了这一点。他身体前倾，说，哦，你付房租有困难吗？我记得这一幕特别清楚，因为那是关于我的房租。这简直离奇地私人化、毫无必要而且毫无征兆，我当时完全被卸下了防备。我不知道该怎么回应。我想我只是结结巴巴地说，不，不是那样的。

00:27:16.890 --> 00:27:26.310
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He wasn't offering to pay her rent directly, just did she need a salary increase to help pay her rent, which Onnesha did not want to talk about.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他并不是主动提出直接帮她付房租，只是问她是否需要增加薪水来帮她付房租，而这是奥内莎根本不想谈论的话题。

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:51.390
<v Onnesha>I don't remember whether I said no, I'm not having trouble paying rent. I think I'd tried to avoid answering that, because I didn't want-- I felt like that was none of his business. But when I tried to shift the conversation back to this is about being compensated, I don't remember if he got angry angry, but he was definitely visibly displeased. And he did not want to give me a raise.
<v Onnesha>我不记得我当时有没有说“不，我付房租没有困难”。我想我曾试图避开回答这个问题，因为我不想——我觉得这根本不管他的事。但当我试图把话题转回“这是关于获得应有报酬”时，我不记得他是不是真的生气了，但他绝对是面露不悦。而且他根本不想给我加薪。

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:54.160
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Instead, he offered her a title change.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>相反，他提出给她更换一个头衔。

