00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:30.270
<v Deanna>And then he-- the bedroom was sort of right on the other side of the kitchen, and he goes into the kitchen and just starts slamming stuff, and throwing stuff, and I'm just hearing shit smashing in the other room, and I am naked in some dude's bed in fucking Big Sur. I don't know how to get out. (VOICE BREAKING) I'm sorry. And eventually, it just stopped. I just sat there the whole time, like I didn't know.
<v Deanna>然后他——卧室刚好在厨房的另一头，他走进厨房，开始摔东西、扔东西，我只听到隔壁房间里东西碎裂的声音，而我正赤身裸体地躺在某个男人的床上，就在他妈的大苏尔。我都不知道该怎么逃出去。（声音哽咽）对不起。最后，声音终于停了。我一动不动地坐在那里，脑子里一片空白。

00:32:30.270 --> 00:32:31.630
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You were in the bed?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你当时在床上？

00:32:31.630 --> 00:32:57.820
<v Deanna>Yeah, I just stayed in the bed. He came back eventually and slept with his back to me, which was like his cold shoulder move. And I just laid there thinking like, I don't know what's going to happen next. Because I really didn't want to challenge him. I just had to start making sense of everything around me, and being OK with everything.
<v Deanna>对，我一直呆在床上。他最后回来了，背对着我睡下，这就好像是他的冷暴力大招。我只是躺在那里想着，我不知道接下来会发生什么。因为我真的不想挑战他。我只能开始试着理解我周围的一切，并且试着接受这一切。

00:32:57.820 --> 00:33:02.580
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So if you're not going to challenge him or talk to him about it, then how are you going to make sense of it?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>既然你既不打算挑战他，也不打算跟他谈谈，那你究竟要怎么去理解它？

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:15.810
<v Deanna>[SOFT CHUCKLE] Shut this out, don't think about this again, and do whatever you can to not make it happen again.
<v Deanna>[轻笑] 屏蔽掉，再也不去想它，然后尽你所能避免让它再次发生。

00:33:15.810 --> 00:33:30.720
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Deanna would manage, manage her feelings, his temper, manage them away from explosive moments. This is what it meant to be close to someone. Managing a relationship was a role Deanna had practice in. It made sense to her.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>迪娜会去管理，管理她的情绪，管理他的脾气，把它们从爆发的边缘拉回来。这就是与某人亲密相处的含义。经营一段关系是迪娜擅长的角色。这在她看来是合情合理的。

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:47.820
<v Deanna>I have been told my whole life that I was going to have difficult relationships with men, that nothing, none of this was going to be easy. They warned me that this was how my relationships were going to be. They were going to be very difficult and challenging, and so this was like, OK. That was how it was going to be for me.
<v Deanna>我一辈子都被灌输这样一种观念：我和男人的关系将会非常艰难，这一切、所有这些绝不会轻松。他们警告过我，我的恋爱关系将会是这样的。它们会非常艰难、充满挑战，所以这就好像，好吧。我的生活注定就是这样。

00:33:47.820 --> 00:34:07.340
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It took three years for Deana to break up with Don after that. She wasn't angry when she did, just tired and sad. She told him she loved him, but she couldn't keep doing this. He wasn't angry either. She thought he would be, but he was gentle. He said he knew the day would come. They both cried.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在那之后，迪娜花了三年时间才和唐分手。她提出分手时并没有生气，只是感到疲惫和难过。她告诉他她爱他，但她实在无法继续下去了。他也没有生气。她以为他会生气的，但他很温柔。他说他知道这一天会到来。他们俩都哭了。

00:34:07.340 --> 00:34:16.880
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>After that, she'd still run into him at professional or social events. They were friendly. A few years after they broke up, Deanna went to a protest uptown.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在那之后，她还是会在职业或社交场合偶遇他。他们依然很友好。分手几年后，迪娜去上城参加了一场抗议活动。

00:34:16.880 --> 00:34:34.820
<v Deanna>And my friends were staging part of the protest, so I went to be supportive. And I ran into Don there. And he was like, well, this is something. You know, we were just kind of, like, chit-chatting. I hadn't seen him in a while. And you know, whatever, whatever. And we're kind of walking along with the protest, and talking to different people.
<v Deanna>我的朋友们正在筹办抗议活动的一部分，所以我去以示支持。我在那儿碰到了唐。他说，哟，这倒挺巧。你知道的，我们当时就是，差不多像是在闲聊。我有一段时间没见他了。然后，随便吧，随便吧。我们一边顺着抗议游行的队伍往前走，一边和不同的人说话。

00:34:34.820 --> 00:34:42.080
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>There was a young woman walking nearby with a reporter's notepad. She was looking around and quickly scribbling notes.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>附近有一个年轻女子走过，手里拿着记者的记事本。她四处张望，飞快地记着笔记。

00:34:42.080 --> 00:35:08.210
<v Deanna>And I've actually watched, like, it felt like watching the Terminator a little bit, he just looked and zeroed in on her, and just bee lined over to her and said, who are you writing for? And she's like, oh, you know, I'm a student. And he's like, you don't have a place for this article yet? I'll give you a place for-- you know, do you want to pitch me? And she was like, who are you you? And he introduces himself, and says AlterNet. She was like, oh, it would be great to write for AlterNet.
<v Deanna>我当时甚至看着他——感觉有点像在看《终结者》，他看了一眼，直接锁定了她，径直朝她走过去说，你是为谁写稿的？她回答说，哦，你知道的，我是个学生。他说，你还没给这篇文章找好发表的地方吗？我给你找个地方发——你知道的，你想向我投稿吗？她就说，你到底是谁？他自我介绍说，AlterNet。她说，哦，能为AlterNet写稿就太棒了。

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:10.650
<v Kristen>He really just came out of nowhere.
<v Kristen>他真的就这么凭空出现了。

00:35:10.650 --> 00:35:22.130
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is Kristen. Kristen was a determined 21-year-old who bought the same breakfast sandwich from the same cafe everyday, because she knew what she liked and she had no need for wasting time.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这位是克里斯汀。克里斯汀是一个坚定的21岁女孩，每天都在同一家咖啡馆买同一种早餐三明治，因为她知道自己喜欢什么，而且从不浪费时间。

00:35:22.130 --> 00:35:29.690
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Kristen had never actually heard of AlterNet, but she knew she wanted to be a reporter, and this guy had a business card and she didn't.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>克里斯汀其实从未听说过AlterNet，但她知道自己想当一名记者，而这家伙有张名片，她却没有。

00:35:29.690 --> 00:35:34.100
<v Kristen>I was a journalism student undergrad at NYU.
<v Kristen>我是纽约大学本科的新闻系学生。

00:35:34.100 --> 00:35:43.400
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Don told her to send him her article, and if it was good, he'd publish it. Kristen said she definitely would. Deanna was off to the side, watching.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>唐让她把文章发给他，如果写得好，他就会发表。克里斯汀说她一定会的。迪娜在一旁看着。

00:35:43.400 --> 00:36:12.610
<v Deanna>And I got so mad, because it just seemed so gross. And I heard that tone of voice. And I had to remembered how he had said things like that to me, like, I'll give you a job, you know, like, I'll help you. I'll support you. And I got so angry, I just looked at him, I was like, I can't fucking believe you. And he was like, what? And I was like, I'm out of here. I'm done.
<v Deanna>我气坏了，因为这看起来太恶心了。我听到了那种语气。我不得不回想起他是怎么对我说过类似的话的，比如，我给你一份工作，你知道的，比如，我来帮。我来支持你。我气极了，我直勾勾地看着他，我说，我他妈真不敢相信你。他说，什么？我说，我走了。我受够了。

00:36:12.610 --> 00:36:23.630
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The story they had created together had just been obliterated. They weren't co-creators. This was his story. Deanna was a set piece.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们一起创造的故事刚刚被彻底抹去了。他们根本不是共同创造者。这是他的故事。迪娜只是一个布景。

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:40.200
<v Deanna>It made me so sick to my stomach. I wasn't that special. And seeing her kind of brightness and enthusiasm, like, wow, someone wants to give me an opportunity, cool. I was so angry. I was so angry. Oh, God.
<v Deanna>这让我恶心得胃里翻江倒海。我根本没那么特殊。看到她那种闪光的热情，就像，哇，有人要给我提供机会，太酷了。我太生气了。我气炸了。噢，天哪。

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:50.880
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Deanna left, went home. Kristen went home, too. She wrote and rewrote her article until it was 2:00 in the morning and she knew she had to get it off to Don.
