00:36:50.880 --> 00:37:02.030
<v Kristen>And I was excited. Yeah, I thought, wow, this is a crazy chance incident, and maybe this will be good, and turn into something, and it did turn into something.
<v Kristen>我很兴奋。是的，我想，哇，这是一次疯狂的巧合，也许这会很棒，能演变成某种成果，而且它确实演变成了某种成果。

00:37:02.030 --> 00:37:11.610
<v Kristen>[SAD LAUGH]
<v Kristen>[悲伤的笑]

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:26.040
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Coming up, the story starts over, but it's going to go so differently next time. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>接下来，故事将重新开始，但下一次将会走向完全不同的方向。芝加哥公共广播电台的节目稍后继续，一分钟后见。

00:37:26.040 --> 00:37:44.550
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's This American Life. I'm Chana Joffe-Walt. Today's show, "Five Women." We wanted to take one of the Me Too stories, where you hear about encounters women have with men who abuse their power in the workplace, and expand that to look at the lives of the women before that encounter.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这里是《美国生活》。我是恰娜·乔夫-沃尔特。今天的节目是“五个女人”。我们想要选取一个“我也是”运动的故事——你在其中听到女性与那些在职场中滥用职权的男性遭遇的经历——并将其扩大，去审视这些女性在遭遇那次事件之前的生活。

00:37:44.550 --> 00:38:04.450
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And we're telling the story of several women who worked for the same person, Don Hazen, at AlterNet over the course of 13 years. So we met Deanna and Onnesha, and we just met Kristen, the student at NYU. Kristen is going to come back. You need to meet someone else first.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们在讲述几个曾在AlterNet为同一个人（唐·哈森）工作了13年的女性的故事。所以我们见过了迪娜和奥内莎，我们刚刚也见过了克里斯汀，那个在纽约大学的学生。克里斯汀还会回来。你得先去认识另一个人。

00:38:04.450 --> 00:38:07.290
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Act 5, "Tana.".
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>第五幕，“塔娜”。

00:38:07.290 --> 00:38:24.810
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Tana was asked to have an opinion about Anita Hill. It was 1991, and she was in the third grade. Her opinion was that it was wrong, because she knew that was the opinion she was supposed to have. The opinion Tana actually had was not one she imagined the adults were asking for.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>塔娜曾被要求对安妮塔·希尔事件发表看法。那是1991年，她当时在读三年级。她的看法是那是错误的，因为她知道那是她“应该”拥有的看法。而塔娜实际拥有的看法并不是她想象中成年人们想要的答案。

00:38:24.810 --> 00:38:39.180
<v Tana>I remember thinking that it wasn't that big of a deal, but I think that my natural instinct-- and I mean, again, I was a child --was maybe people were freaking out a little bit too much. Maybe everyone should be a little bit more chill.
<v Tana>我记得当时觉得这没那么大不了，但我认为我的自然本能——我的意思是，再次强调，我当时还是个孩子——可能是觉得大家有点大惊小怪了。也许所有人都该更冷静一点。

00:38:39.180 --> 00:39:05.190
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>By 10th grade, she was stronger in her conviction. She knew about sex, and she knew about feminism, and she believed that if Monica Lewinsky wanted to have sex with someone, or blow them, or whatever actually happened in the Oval Office, that was her choice. Men had been allowed to follow their bliss forever. They went a little too far, but that had been corrected, and now Tana could see things had swung too far in the other direction.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>到了10年级，她的信念更加坚定了。她懂得了性，懂得了女权主义，她坚信如果莫妮卡·莱温斯基想要和某人发生性关系，或者给他口交，或者椭圆形办公室里实际发生的事情不管是啥，那是她自己的选择。男人们一直被允许永远追求他们的快乐。他们可能走得稍微有点远，但那已经被纠正了，而现在塔娜能看出事情已经向另一个方向摆得太过了。

00:39:05.190 --> 00:39:22.230
<v Tana>Oh, nobody can have sex anymore. That's terrible. That was the impression I got. Like, I just envisioned the US workplace as a bunch of bureaucratic, fun-hating HR people taking you to jail if you said someone looked nice.
<v Tana>噢，再也不能做爱了。这太可怕了。这就是我当时的印象。比如，我脑海中的美国职场就是一群官僚主义的、痛恨乐趣的人力资源人员，只要你夸某人看起来不错，就会把你抓进监狱。

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:31.540
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>One of her first bosses was Jeff. He ran a shop in town. Tana had just started college, but it was summer, so she spent most of her days at the shop.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她的第一任老板之一是杰夫。他在镇上经营一家店铺。塔娜刚开始上大学，但正值暑假，所以她大部分时间都在店里度过。

00:39:31.540 --> 00:39:51.760
<v Tana>And he would always tell me these awful things about his wife, where he'd say, you know, she's so beautiful, when she walks down a restaurant, all men, you know, whiplash to check her out, but I'm just sick of her. Want to go out to dinner, can I get you a sandwich? You know, like, you're so tiny. Can I grab your waist? He definitely tried to physically grab me a lot.
<v Tana>他总是对我说他妻子那些可怕的事情，他会说，你知道的，她太漂亮了，当她走过餐厅时，所有的男人，你知道的，都会猛回头去偷看她，但我实在看够她了。想出去吃晚饭吗，要不要我给你买个三明治？你知道的，比如，你太娇小了。我能抓一下你的腰吗？他绝对试图在身体上多次抓我。

00:39:51.760 --> 00:40:20.190
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It was irritating, but Tana tried really hard not to show it because Jeff was middle-aged, miserable, she'd heard his wife kicked him out. A coworker said he found Jeff in the shop one morning, waking up surrounded by beer bottles. He made Tana uncomfortable, but he was so pitiful that it felt unreasonable to attach any value to her discomfort. Like when her friend at work, a guy her age, told her, Jeff always talks about you. It's really creepy.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这很烦人，但塔娜竭力不表现出来，因为杰夫已经中年、生活悲惨，她听说他妻子把他赶出去了。一个同事说他有一天早上在店里发现杰夫，在一堆啤酒瓶中醒来。他让塔娜感到不舒服，但他又是如此可怜，以至于把她的不适感当回事显得很不合情理。就像当她在公司里一个同龄的男性朋友对她说，杰夫总是谈论你。这真的很让人毛骨悚然。

00:40:20.190 --> 00:40:29.910
<v Tana>And he goes, [SIGHING] Jeff always talks to me about how he just wants to put his head between your breasts, and just go
<v Tana>然后他说，[叹气]杰夫总是跟我念叨，说他只想把头埋进你的胸部中间，然后发出——

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:31.390
<v Tana>[EXHALING AIR THROUGH CLOSED LIPS]
<v Tana>[嘴唇紧闭呼气]

00:40:31.390 --> 00:40:43.590
<v Tana>Like the motorboating sound, and this guy said that. And I was like, are you kidding me? That's the saddest thing. That's what a 12-year-old would say. This is a grown-ass man. How could he say that? I just remember being, like, so grossed out.
